    "q-164424": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164424",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(4,2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $2$ and $6.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
